## The article in one breath

This is the "boring, most-important chapter": the aerobic **base** (the floor) is the master system, and you build it *before* the VO2-max **ceiling**. Three energy systems run as a continuous mix (always on, the blend shifts with duration); the anaerobic pair are a *sprint budget* and the aerobic system is the *salary that refills it*. The practical core: find Zone 2 by feel, train **polarized 80/20** (easy easy, hard hard, nothing in the Zone-3 grey zone), run **two protocols for two jobs** (steady Zone 2 for the floor, Norwegian 4×4 for the ceiling), and read the engine by **convergence** — resting HR down while VO2-max estimate and Zone-2 pace climb together.

The hero insight to make instantly legible: **your real-world fitness is the usable space *between* a floor and a ceiling — and the floor is the part you actually get to raise, for years.**

---

## 1. The Big Idea

> One poster that makes the reader *feel* this: fitness is not a single number you chase — it's the **height of a room** between a floor you can keep raising (the aerobic base) and a ceiling that barely moves (VO2 max). Build the floor first; the room grows from the bottom up.

The reader should walk away understanding (a) floor-before-ceiling as an *order of operations*, and (b) that "the engine getting bigger" is a convergence of several gauges, not one heroic stat.

